{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Shiggaion</b> — Some interpret it as 'delight,' like תשגה תמיד. And some say: concerning his unintentional sin. But the correct view in my eyes is that it concerns the beauty of a poem whose opening word is shiggaion." "<b>Concerning the words of Cush</b> — Just as the composers of piyyutim in Spain write at the top of the beginning of the piyyut's column, the theme of a known piyyut." "<b>Concerning the words of Cush the Benjaminite</b> — Some say: that he is Saul, who was beautiful, in the manner of: כי אשה כושית לקח. And this is far-fetched in my eyes, that they would lower a word of praise to disparagement, and this is the opposite of סגיא נהור. And what the Rabbis, blessed be their memory, said: that they did not mention him for a blessing, so that the evil eye would not have power over him — it is very far-fetched. And the correct view is that this Cush was a Jew and that was his name, and he was of the tribe of Benjamin, for it is far-fetched that he would say about Saul, the chosen of YHWH: הנה יחבל און."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:26:26.667944356Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}